          "type": "user",
          "text": "# Lustre SSG Usage Guide\n\n## Installation\n\nAdd lustre_ssg as a development dependency:\n```\ngleam add lustre_ssg --dev\n```\n\n## Project Structure\n\nCreate a `build.gleam` file in your project's `src` directory to serve as your build script.\n\n## Configuration Overview\n\nAccording to the documentation, \"lustre_ssg is a low-config static site generator for simple things like a personal blog or documentation site.\"\n\nThe generator operates on two core concepts:\n\n**Static Routes**: Fixed pages that render the same content every time\n**Dynamic Routes**: Pages generated from a data source, creating multiple pages from a template\n\n## Basic Setup Example\n\nHere's the fundamental pattern:\n\n```gleam\nimport lustre/ssg\n\npub fn main() {\n  let build = ssg.new(\"./priv\")\n    |> ssg.add_static_route(\"/\", index.view())\n    |> ssg.add_dynamic_route(\"/blog\", posts, post.view)\n    |> ssg.build\n\n  case build {\n    Ok(_) -> io.println(\"Build succeeded!\")\n    Error(e) -> io.debug(e)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Key Components\n\n- **Output Directory**: Specified in `ssg.new()` (e.g., `\"./priv\"`)\n- **Static Routes**: Use `add_static_route(path, rendered_html)`\n- **Dynamic Routes**: Use `add_dynamic_route(path_prefix, data_dict, render_function)`\n\n## Building Your Site\n\nExecute the build script with:\n```\ngleam run -m build\n```\n\n## Important Limitations\n\nThe project explicitly notes it \"is not a batteries-included framework for generating static sites. There is no CLI, no built-in server, no fancy data fetching, and no client-side hydration.\" You must build these features yourself if needed.\n\n## Output Structure\n\nGenerated files organize into directories matching your routes, with `index.html` files for directory paths.",
